  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 311 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An upper level low slowly builds into the region today \nand tomorrow, bringing several rounds of showers as shortwaves pivot \naround the longwave trough. Instability will reach its maximum today \nand some of the stronger showers will likely contain thunder. Pea \nsized hail will be possible, and maybe graupel in the higher \nterrain. The highest chances for showers will be farther north where \nthe better dynamics and coldest air aloft will be. A lowering \ninversion heading into Friday should prevent any thunder chances, \nwith instability only reaching up to around 10,000 feet and not \nsignificantly surpassing -10 Celsius. However, the airmass will \ncontinue to cool and temperatures at the summits are expected to \nfall below freezing on Friday, so a few flurries/snow showers will \nbe possible in the mountains, especially farther north. Snow levels \nlook to drop to around 3,500-4,000 feet. Flow remains unblocked \nheading through Friday as the inversion will stay well above summit \nlevel.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Surface high pressure builds in for the weekend, leading \nto a couple classic fall days. Highs will be in the 50s and 60s with \nmostly sunny skies. Radiational cooling conditions will become \nsomewhat favorable for Friday night, but some lingering boundary \nflow may prevent the most efficient temperature decreases. The \nboundary layer looks to decouple at least in the later part of the \nnight though. Frost formation is expected across most of the region, \nbut it looks more uncertain in the Champlain Valley, the only area \nleft in the growing season. Temperatures there will be marginal and \nwinds there will likely hold on the longest.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: The remnants of Isaias will bring some rain to southern \nportions of our forecast area Sunday night into Monday. Not \nexpecting any strong winds with this system. Northern extent of \nprecipitation is uncertain. For now have highest chances for \nprecipitation from Saranac Lake NY to Montpelier VT and areas south \nof there.",
